    "title": "pandas.Series.set_axis — pandas 3.1.0.dev0+1661.gf74cda2e6e documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.Series.set_axis#\n\n- \nSeries.set_axis(*labels* ,*** ,*axis=0* ,*copy=<no_default>* )[source]#\n- Assign desired index to given axis. Deprecated since version 3.0.0: This keyword is ignored and will be removed in pandas 4.0. Since pandas 3.0, this method always returns a new object using a lazy copy mechanism that defers copies until necessary (Copy-on-Write). See the user guide on Copy-on-Write for more details. Indexes for row labels can be changed by assigning a list-like or Index. \n  - Parameters:\n    - **labels** list-like or Index\n    - The values for the new index.\n    - **axis** {0 or ‘index’}, default 0\n    - The axis to update. The value 0 identifies the rows. For Series this parameter is unused and defaults to 0.\n    - **copy** bool, default False\n    - This keyword is now ignored; changing its value will have no impact on the method.\n  - Returns:\n    - Series\n    - A shallow copy of the object with axis altered to the given index.\n See also \n  - `Series.rename_axis`\n  - Alter the name of the index.\n Examples >>> s = pd.Series([1, 2, 3]) >>> s 0 1 1 2 2 3 dtype: int64 >>> s.set_axis([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"], axis=0) a 1 b 2 c 3 dtype: int64",
    "markdown_length": 1166,
